# @corpus current
# ADJACENCY is the whole rule: `>>=` is `>>` with an `=` glued to it — two
# tokens, no third lexical identity. `src/parser.zig` reads it that way.
#
# THE FILE NAMED FOR THAT LAW STOPPED CARRYING IT. Commit 98ceb52c, an
# argument-projection migration whose message never mentions compound
# assignment, expanded all four glued operators here into `a = a >> 3`. The
# oracle line did not move — both spellings answer 8 — so no number could see
# it, and nothing ran this file. `idol fmt` performs the same expansion today,
# which is why gate/layout.sh checks that the FACE is still here and not only
# that the numbers agree.
#
# BOTH SPELLINGS APPEAR AND THE ORACLE REQUIRES THEM TO AGREE: `a`..`d` glued,
# `x`..`w` the expanded form it replaces, over identical operands. That
# agreement is the equivalence witness the update-of-place law demands before
# the compound form may be called canonical.
#
# The operands separate the operators — 64>>3 = 8, 1<<4 = 16, 8|5 = 13,
# 12&10 = 8 — so a wrong mapping cannot land on the right number.
#
# `e` is the negative control: a bare `e >> 1` is still an EXPRESSION and
# leaves `e` at 5. The spaced form `a >> = 3` refuses with "expected
# expression, got '='"; the gate runs that refusal rather than asserting it.
# expect: 8 16 13 8 5 8 16 13 8

a = 64
a >>= 3
b = 1
b <<= 4
c = 8
c |= 5
d = 12
d &= 10
e = 5
e >> 1
x = 64
x = x >> 3
y = 1
y = y << 4
z = 8
z = z | 5
w = 12
w = w & 10
print("{a} {b} {c} {d} {e} {x} {y} {z} {w}")
0
